C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not gather what churches say about apostles and prophets and leave the reader to choose. It says what the author believes the Scriptures state about the two words, it sets the passages in the order they stand, and it answers the strongest objections from named modern writers, so that a reader who disagrees knows where the disagreement lies."),
("p", "The question is narrow. The New Testament names apostles and prophets, lists them first among the gifts God has set in the church, and says that the household of God is “built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets” (Ephesians 2:20). Do those offices continue, and what do the passages mean by each word? The question is asked of the passages that name the persons and the offices. It is not asked of the gifts of tongues, prophecy as a gift in the assembly or healing, which are the subject of the neighbouring volumes."),
("p", "The words need sorting before the question can be answered, because the New Testament uses “apostle” of more than one kind of person. The Lord “chose twelve, whom also he named apostles” (Luke 6:13). Paul writes “Am I not an apostle?” and answers it from a sight of the Lord and from the churches he founded (1 Corinthians 9:1, 2). Barnabas is called an apostle beside Paul (Acts 14:14). Epaphroditus is called “your messenger,” and the Greek word behind that rendering is the word for an apostle (Philippians 2:25). Andronicus and Junia are “of note among the apostles” (Romans 16:7). The word for a prophet is likewise used of Agabus, of the daughters of Philip and, in Titus 1:12, of a poet of Crete."),
("p", "The position is this. “Apostle” names three kinds of person in the New Testament: the Twelve, Paul, and sent ones such as Barnabas and Epaphroditus. “Prophet” names persons in the assemblies of the overlap, and the prophets of Ephesians 2:20 and 3:5 are those to whom the mystery was revealed now, not the Twelve, not the prophets of the Old Testament and not the prophets of Israel’s Kingdom. The foundation of Ephesians 2:20 is the revelation of the mystery, laid once, with Jesus Christ himself as the chief corner stone. No verse names a successor to an apostle or to a prophet. The apostolic office as a sign-authenticated witness ends with the sign-program at Acts 28, and Paul’s authority continues in the letters he wrote after the record of Acts closes. The Twelve’s office is suspended with Israel’s program at Acts 28 and resumes in the Kingdom."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, each chapter opens with a claim, reads its passages in order, and closes with a short table and a list of what the volume takes from the passages, so that the reader can see what has been argued and test it. Third, where a modern writer is named, the volume says what that writer holds, in its own words, and gives the writer’s words only where it has read them in a free full text."),
("p", "The volume does not take up what its neighbours argue. The purpose of signs and the last sign recorded in Acts belong to Volume 1 of this series, tongues and prophecy as gifts to Volume 2, and healing to Volume 3. The order of the assembly and the lists of gifts in 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 belong to Pauline Doctrine Series Volume 4, the choosing of Matthias to Acts Commentary Volume 1, Paul’s call to Acts Commentary Volume 3 and the suspension at Acts 28 to Acts Commentary Volume 5. This volume takes the offices from those neighbours and reads the passages that name the persons."),
("p", "One boundary runs through the volume. The signs and miracles in Acts 9 to 28, those of Paul among them, are overlap phenomena. The volume reports them as the text reports them, does not assign them to Israel’s program and does not convert them into the Mystery. It makes no statement about God’s power."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "The New Testament uses “apostle” of three kinds of person and “prophet” of persons in the assemblies of the overlap. The Twelve were chosen by the Lord, sent to the house of Israel and promised twelve thrones; their office is suspended with Israel’s program at Acts 28 and resumes in the Kingdom. Paul was made an apostle by the risen Lord, with the revelation of the mystery committed to him, and the signs of an apostle were wrought by him; his office as a sign-authenticated witness ends with the sign-program at Acts 28, and his authority continues in the letters written after the record of Acts closes. Barnabas, Epaphroditus and others are sent ones, called apostles in the plain sense of the word, and the volume decides nothing on whether Andronicus and Junia held the office. The prophets of Ephesians 2:20 and 3:5 are prophets of the Mystery, to whom the secret was revealed now. The foundation is the revelation laid once, and no verse names a successor. Ephesians 4:13 states a goal and does not date the offices, and the volume does not extend apostles or prophets beyond Acts 28."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in seven statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("Three kinds of apostle", "The Twelve; Paul; sent ones such as Barnabas and Epaphroditus", "Chapters 2–5"),
    ("The Twelve", "Chosen and sent to Israel, promised twelve thrones; the office is suspended with Israel’s program and resumes in the Kingdom", "Chapters 3, 11"),
    ("Paul", "Made an apostle by the risen Lord; the signs of an apostle were wrought by him", "Chapters 5, 6"),
    ("The signs of an apostle", "The sign-authenticated witness ends with the sign-program at Acts 28; Paul’s authority continues in his letters", "Chapters 6, 11"),
    ("The prophets", "Persons in the assemblies of the overlap; those of Ephesians 2:20 and 3:5 are prophets of the Mystery", "Chapters 8, 9"),
    ("The foundation", "The revelation of the mystery, laid once, with Jesus Christ the chief corner stone; no verse names a successor", "Chapters 9, 11"),
    ("Ephesians 4:13", "A stated goal; the verse dates no office and does not extend apostles or prophets beyond Acts 28", "Chapter 10"),
  ], [0.24, 0.52, 0.24]),
]
